  "text": "שלטון .NotNillaş\n\n}`;\n\n  if (!sidebar) {\n    console.log('Missing sidebar');\n    return;\n  }\n\n  if (!sidebar.mobile) {\n    console.log('Missing sidebar.mobile');\n    return;\n  }\nHere’s a step-by-step breakdown of the problem and its solution:\n\n### Problem Breakdown:\n1. **Function \\( g(x) \\) and its Graph:**\n   - The function \\( g(x) \\) is defined on the interval \\([-4, 4]\\).\n   - Its graph is **symmetric with respect to the origin**, meaning \\( g(-x) = -g(x) \\) for all \\( x \\) in \\([-4, 4]\\).\n   - The graph consists of **8 line segments**, each with a slope of \\( 1 \\) or \\( -1 \\).\n   - The graph has **7 turning points**, and the \\( y \\)-coordinate of each turning point is a **distinct integer**.\n\n2. **Objective:**\n   - Determine the sum of all integers \\( d \\) for which the equation \\( g(x) = d \\) has **exactly 4 solutions**.\n\n### Solution Steps:\n1. **Understanding Symmetry:**\n   - Since \\( g(x) \\) is symmetric with respect to the origin, for each positive \\( x \\), there is a corresponding negative \\( x \\) that flips the graph.\n   - This symmetry likely helps in understanding the number of solutions for \\( g(x) = d \\).\n\n2. **Turning Points:**\n   - The function \\( g(x) \\) has **7 turning points**, each with a distinct integer \\( y \\)-coordinate.\n   - Turning points are typically where the function changes direction (from increasing to decreasing or vice versa).\n\n3. **Equation \\( g(x) = d \\):**\n   - We need to find all integers \\( d \\) for which the equation \\( g(x) = d \\) has exactly 4 solutions.\n   - Since \\( g(x) \\) is symmetric with respect to the origin, the equation \\( g(x) = d \\) will have solutions for \\( x \\) in \\([-4, 4]\\).\n\n4. **Analyzing the Graph:**\n   - The graph of \\( g(x) \\) has 8 line segments with slopes \\( \\pm 1 \\).\n   - These segments are likely the edges of the graph, connecting the up to the right, up to the left, down to the right, and down to the left.\n   - The turning points are likely the intersections of these line segments, yielding distinct \\( y \\)-coordinates.\n\n5. **Determining \\( d \\):**\n   - For \\( g(x) = d \\) to have exactly 4 solutions:\n     - The \\( y \\)-coordinate \\( d \\) must correspond to a point where the graph intersects the horizontal line \\( y = d \\).\n     - Given the symmetry and the number of turning points, \\( d \\) must be one of the distinct \\( y \\)-coordinates where the graph passes through from one side to the other and back.\n\n6. **Identifying Valid \\( d \\):**\n   - The integer \\( d \\) values that allow exactly 4 solutions are likely the \\( y \\)-coordinates at the boundaries of each segment.\n   - Given there are 7 turning points with distinct integer \\( y \\)-coordinates, the possible \\( d \\) values are the \\( y \\)-coordinates of the highest and lowest turning points.\n\n7. **Summing the Valid \\( d \\) Values:**\n   - Suppose the distinct \\( y \\)-coordinates are \\( d_1, d_2, d_3, d_4, d_5, d_6, d_7 \\).\n   - The equation \\( d \\) for which \\( g(x) = d \\) has exactly 4 solutions are \\( d_1, d_2, d_3, d_4 \\).\n   - The sum of these \\( d \\) values would be \\( d_1 + d_2 + d_3 + d_4 \\).\n\n### Final Answer:\nGiven the symmetry and the number of turning points, the sum of all integers \\( d \\) for which \\( g(x) = d \\) has exactly 4 solutions is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]\n\nThis conclusion is based on the fact that the sum of the \\( y \\)-coordinates of the turning points where the graph intersects from one side to the other and back results in a net sum of zero (since the positive and negative \\( y \\)-coordinates cancel out).",
